REV.page({"course":"english1","id":"w08","title":"Syllables, word stress, plurals and aspiration","summary":"The hidden sound patterns of English words. Every word is built from syllables, and one syllable carries the stress; a few patterns predict which one. A plural ending sounds /s/, /z/ or /iz/ depending on the last sound of the word, and p, t and k get a puff of air at the start of a stressed syllable. These patterns help you follow other speakers and speak more clearly.","n":8,"lectures":[{"code":"8.1","title":"Stress in words | rules, patterns & practice for clear english pronunciation","video":"8gud9XJsL6k","seconds":2084},{"code":"8.2","title":"Plurality in english | rules & pronunciation tips for forming plurals correctly","video":"42hufxqiOAk","seconds":1414},{"code":"8.3","title":"Aspiration in english words | rules, stress patterns & tips for clear pronunciation","video":"IoprN0REcgs","seconds":2002},{"code":"8.4","title":"Syllables & stress in words | rules, exceptions & tips for clear english pronunciation","video":"6SrSSSEHWxk","seconds":1198},{"code":"8.5","title":"Aspiration, stress & syllabification | pronunciation rules & practice for clear spoken english","video":"LdteKj-oE5I","seconds":1142}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"syllables","level":2,"title":"Syllables"},{"id":"words-are-not-random-strings-of-sounds","level":3,"title":"Words are not random strings of sounds"},{"id":"what-a-syllable-is","level":3,"title":"What a syllable is"},{"id":"counting-syllables","level":3,"title":"Counting syllables"},{"id":"syllabification","level":3,"title":"Syllabification"},{"id":"word-stress","level":2,"title":"Word stress"},{"id":"patterns-not-rules","level":3,"title":"Patterns, not rules"},{"id":"two-syllable-words-nouns-and-adjectives-first-verbs-second","level":3,"title":"Two-syllable words: nouns and adjectives first, verbs second"},{"id":"same-spelling-noun-or-verb","level":3,"title":"Same spelling, noun or verb"},{"id":"words-built-from-a-one-syllable-word","level":3,"title":"Words built from a one-syllable word"},{"id":"compound-words-phrasal-verbs-and-reflexive-pronouns","level":3,"title":"Compound words, phrasal verbs and reflexive pronouns"},{"id":"endings-that-decide-the-stress","level":3,"title":"Endings that decide the stress"},{"id":"prefixes","level":3,"title":"Prefixes"},{"id":"when-you-are-not-sure","level":3,"title":"When you are not sure"},{"id":"plurals-s-z-and-iz","level":2,"title":"Plurals: /s/, /z/ and /iz/"},{"id":"spelling-first-then-sound","level":3,"title":"Spelling first, then sound"},{"id":"the-rule","level":3,"title":"The rule"},{"id":"why-s-and-z-and-why-p-t-k","level":3,"title":"Why /s/ and /z/, and why /p/, /t/, /k/"},{"id":"aspiration","level":2,"title":"Aspiration"},{"id":"take-a-piece-of-cake","level":3,"title":"Take a piece of cake"},{"id":"the-voiceless-stops-p-t-k","level":3,"title":"The voiceless stops /p/, /t/, /k/"},{"id":"one-syllable-words","level":3,"title":"One-syllable words"},{"id":"longer-words-the-stressed-syllable","level":3,"title":"Longer words: the stressed syllable"},{"id":"the-rules-to-remember","level":3,"title":"The rules to remember"},{"id":"practice-from-lecture-8-5","level":2,"title":"Practice from Lecture 8.5"},{"id":"how-many-syllables","level":3,"title":"How many syllables?"},{"id":"consonant-clusters","level":3,"title":"Consonant clusters"},{"id":"mark-the-stress","level":3,"title":"Mark the stress"},{"id":"which-words-are-aspirated","level":3,"title":"Which words are aspirated?"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Syllables</strong> (8.1, 8.4): a syllable is a unit of a word, built around a <strong>vowel sound</strong>, its nucleus. Count vowel <em>sounds</em>, not vowel letters: <em>boys</em> has one syllable. The jaw-drop test counts syllables for you.</li>\n<li><strong>Stress</strong> (8.1, 8.4): in a word of two or more syllables, <strong>one syllable is more prominent</strong> than the others: said with more force, a little louder and longer. A word has one stress, and it falls on a vowel.</li>\n<li><strong>Stress patterns</strong> (8.1, 8.4): two-syllable nouns and adjectives → first syllable (<em>TI-ger</em>); two-syllable verbs and prepositions → second (<em>be-GIN</em>, <em>a-MONG</em>); so <em>an OB-ject</em> but <em>to ob-JECT</em>. Compound nouns → first part (<em>BOOK-shop</em>). Many suffixes put the stress just before them (<em>e-du-CA-tion</em>, <em>un-der-STAND-a-ble</em>); <em>-ee</em>, <em>-oo</em> and <em>-ique</em> take it themselves (<em>a-GREE</em>, <em>sham-POO</em>, <em>u-NIQUE</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>When in doubt, do not stress</strong> (8.1): no stress sounds better than a wrong stress. A dictionary marks the stressed syllable with ˈ.</li>\n<li><strong>Plurals</strong> (8.2): listen to the <strong>last sound</strong> of the singular. After /p/, /t/ or /k/ the plural sounds <strong>/s/</strong> (<em>caps, cats, books</em>); everywhere else it sounds <strong>/z/</strong> (<em>dogs, babies, windows</em>); after the last sound of <em>judge</em>, <em>bench</em> or <em>rose</em> a short vowel comes in: <strong>/iz/</strong> (<em>judges</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Aspiration</strong> (8.3, 8.5): the voiceless stops /p/, /t/, /k/ get a <strong>puff of air</strong>, written [pʰ], [tʰ], [kʰ], at the start of a <strong>stressed syllable</strong> (<em>take, piece, cake; po-TA-to</em>). No puff after /s/ (<em>stop</em>), at the start of an unstressed syllable, or at the end of a syllable. Voiced stops (b, d, g) never get one.</li>\n<li><strong>Indian English</strong> (8.3): usually does not aspirate, and that is a variety, not a mistake. Learn the patterns to understand other varieties and to choose how you speak.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"syllables\">Syllables</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 8.1 starts from what we already know about words. A word is a <strong>group of sounds</strong>, and the sounds come in a fixed order, not at random. English has only <strong>44 sounds</strong>, 24 consonants and 20 vowels (<a href=\"#/english1/w01/the-44-sounds-of-english\">Week 1</a>), yet from them it builds an endless number of words. “Doesn’t it sound miraculous?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). Between a sound and a word there is a middle unit, the <strong>syllable</strong>. You need syllables to understand stress, and you need stress to understand aspiration. That is the plan of this week.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"words-are-not-random-strings-of-sounds\">Words are not random strings of sounds</h3>\n<p>The lecture reminds us of the patterns from <a href=\"#/english1/w01/consonant-clusters\">Week 1</a> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a consonant and a vowel taking turns (consonant, vowel, consonant, vowel …) is the most frequent pattern;</li>\n<li>two consonants together are fine, but less frequent;</li>\n<li>three consonants together are rare, but still possible;</li>\n<li>four consonants together at the start of a word are not possible at all;</li>\n<li>heavy clusters of consonants anywhere in a word are avoided.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(A <strong>cluster</strong> is a group of consonant sounds with no vowel between them, like <em>spr</em> in <em>spring</em>.)</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-a-syllable-is\">What a syllable is</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Syllable</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>“A <strong>syllable</strong> is a unit in a word” (8.1, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>): “the unit in which the word is divided while pronouncing it” (8.4, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>).</li>\n<li>“Syllables have <strong>nucleus</strong> (most often a vowel) with optional initial and final <strong>margins</strong> (typically, consonants).” The <strong>nucleus</strong> is the centre of the syllable. The <strong>margins</strong> are the sounds before it (initial) and after it (final).</li>\n<li>“Every syllable has to have a vowel, but not necessarily a consonant” (8.4).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Take <em>sun</em>, one of the lecture’s one-syllable words: the vowel sound in the middle is the nucleus, and the consonants s and n are its margins.</p>\n<p>The vowel is the heart of the syllable (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Almost always, the nucleus is a <strong>vowel sound</strong>. (The lecture adds a footnote: in a few cases a sound that is not a vowel can be the nucleus, but it leaves those details out.)</li>\n<li>So <strong>no syllable is possible without a vowel</strong>, and therefore <strong>no word is possible without a vowel</strong>.</li>\n<li>But a word can be made of vowel sounds <strong>only</strong>, and it can be as small as <strong>one sound</strong> (for example <em>eye</em>, which has only vowel sounds; see <a href=\"#/english1/w01/words-as-patterns-of-c-and-v\">Week 1</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">One, two or many syllables</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>monosyllabic</strong> word has one syllable (<em>mono</em> = one): <em>sun, cat</em>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>disyllabic</strong> word has two (<em>di</em> = two): <em>tiger, begin</em>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>multi-syllable</strong> word has more than one; words with many syllables are also called <em>polysyllabic</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>How many syllables can a word have (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>)? One-syllable words are very many. Words with two, three and four syllables are frequent. Words of five, six or more syllables are rare: technically possible, but “difficult to produce and pronounce”, so long, heavy words are not preferred. Lecture 8.3 says the same: beyond four, the number of possible words drops sharply (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). The 8.4 slide adds: “That’s not all! There are words with many more syllables.”</p>\n<p>The lectures’ example words, with the syllable breaks the slides show (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Syllables</th>\n<th>Lecture 8.1</th>\n<th>Lecture 8.4</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>sun, life, break, tongue</td>\n<td>cat, buy, force, drink, walk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>tiger (tai-gar), police (po-lis), between (bit-wiin), marriage (maer-rij)</td>\n<td>begin (bih-gin), bury (ber-ee), equal (ee-qwul), happy (hap-ee), funny (fun-ee)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>energy (e-ner-jii), capital (kae-pi-tal), heritage (he-ri-tez), suffering (saf-far-ing)</td>\n<td>mosquito (muh-skee-toh), September (sep-tem-ber), department (dih-pahrt-ment), camera (cam-er-a), vitamin (vi-ta-min)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>invisible (in-vi-si-ble), education (e-du-ke-shan), aquarium (a-kwae-rii-am), infinity (in-fi-ni-tii)</td>\n<td>information (in-for-may-shun), January (Jan-yoo-air-ee), American (uh-mer-i-kun), discovery (dih-skuhv-er-ee), dictionary (dik-shuh-ner-ee)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The spellings in brackets are the slides’ own simple respellings of the sounds, not IPA. Each hyphen marks a syllable break. One slip on the 8.1 slide: <em>heritage</em> ends in the j sound of <em>judge</em>, as <em>marriage</em> (maer-rij) does, not in a z sound (he-ri-tij).</p>\n<h3 id=\"counting-syllables\">Counting syllables</h3>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The jaw-drop test</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 8.4 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>): put your hand under your jaw and say the word. Count how many times your jaw drops: that is the number of syllables. <em>Cat, buy, force, drink, walk</em>: the jaw drops once. <em>Be-gin</em>: twice. <em>In-for-ma-tion</em>: four times.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The second test comes from Lecture 8.5 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdteKj-oE5I&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>): make sure that <strong>every syllable has one vowel</strong>, and <strong>only one vowel</strong>. A vowel here means a vowel <strong>sound</strong>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Vowel letters are not vowel sounds</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Boys</em> has two vowel letters (o and y; the slide respells it <em>Boiz</em>), but they make <strong>one</strong> vowel sound, /ɔɪ/, a diphthong (<a href=\"#/english1/w01/diphthongs\">Week 1</a>). So <em>boys</em>, /bɔɪz/, has <strong>one</strong> syllable. “You need to look at vowel sound and not vowel letters” (8.5, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdteKj-oE5I&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>). In the same way <em>know</em>, /nəʊ/, is one syllable.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"syllabification\">Syllabification</h3>\n<p><strong>Syllabification</strong> is dividing a word into its syllables. For native speakers (people who grew up speaking English) it is <strong>intuitive</strong>: they feel the syllables without being taught (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>). That makes it hard for non-native speakers, but “not impossible”: we can <strong>observe the patterns</strong>, bring them into practice, and mature through practice.</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s 100-word exercise</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 8.1 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Take 20 random words. Split each into syllables yourself and count the breaks.</li>\n<li>Check your splits in a dictionary or an online tool that shows syllable boundaries. Expect to get about 10 of 20 right at first.</li>\n<li>Build up to 100 words: 20 words each with one, two, three, four and five syllables. Do it over many days, not in one hour.</li>\n<li>Stage two (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>): for each word, find the stressed syllable, by listening to others or with an online tool, and say the word with the stress in the right place.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>After this, any new word will make you a little “restless” until you know its syllables and its stress, and your speaking improves naturally.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"word-stress\">Word stress</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Say <em>January</em> (Lecture 8.4’s example, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). You do not say its four syllables in the same way: one of them, <em>JAN</em>, comes out with more force. In every word of two or more syllables, one syllable stands out like this. A one-syllable word has nothing to stand out from, so stress becomes a question only from two syllables up (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Stress</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>“Stress means <strong>prominence</strong>”: making one syllable stand out more than the others (8.1, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</li>\n<li>“Syllables uttered with <strong>greater degree of force</strong> is said to be stressed”; the rest are <strong>unstressed</strong> syllables (8.4, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</li>\n<li>In <em>water</em>, “<em>wa</em> is elongated and said a little louder than the next syllable <em>ter</em>” (8.4, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>). So a stressed syllable is said <strong>louder</strong> and <strong>longer</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>That some syllable is stressed is “a rule which has probably no exception” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>). The hard part is knowing <strong>which</strong> one.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The three golden rules of stress</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 8.4 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A word can have only <strong>one</strong> stress.</li>\n<li>Only <strong>vowels</strong> are stressed, not consonants.</li>\n<li>There are <strong>many exceptions</strong> to the rules.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<p>Lecture 8.3 puts rule 2 in one line: “Stress – a feature of vowels in a syllable.”</p>\n<p><strong>How the lectures write stress.</strong> Hyphens split the word into syllables, and the stressed syllable is in CAPITALS (8.1) or in <strong>bold</strong> (8.4). This page uses capitals. The 8.4 slide <strong>Word stress &amp; rules</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Syllables, stressed one in capitals</th>\n<th>Count</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>water</td>\n<td>WA-ter</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>people</td>\n<td>PEO-ple</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>television</td>\n<td>TEL-e-vi-sion</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>together</td>\n<td>to-GETH-er</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>potato</td>\n<td>po-TA-to</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>banana</td>\n<td>ba-NA-na</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>begin</td>\n<td>be-GIN</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Why stress matters</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>When others stress a word differently from the way we say it, the word can sound strange to us, and this “mismatch between listening and speaking” makes understanding harder (8.1, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). Once we notice stress, we stop wondering about it when we listen, and we can fix our own words if we want to. The lecturer calls these <strong>underlying patterns</strong>: patterns that are not obvious until someone shows them, and then hard to miss.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"patterns-not-rules\">Patterns, not rules</h3>\n<p>The 8.1 slide <strong>Stress patterns</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>): “Stress patterns in words are mostly unpredictable in English. However, some general rules can be applied to many words.” Native speakers know the stress by intuition; we learn it by watching them speak. So the patterns below are “observable patterns”: “we cannot take them as rules” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>). Lecture 8.4 says they work for “at least 80 percent of the words in that class” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</p>\n<p>The word classes matter here (<a href=\"#/english1/w02/parts-of-speech\">Week 2</a>): a <strong>noun</strong> names something (<em>tiger</em>); an <strong>adjective</strong> describes a noun (<em>happy</em>); a <strong>verb</strong> shows an action (<em>begin</em>); a <strong>preposition</strong> shows how a noun relates to the rest of the sentence (<em>among</em>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"two-syllable-words-nouns-and-adjectives-first-verbs-second\">Two-syllable words: nouns and adjectives first, verbs second</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two-syllable nouns and adjectives</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“With most of the disyllabic nouns and adjectives, stress is on the <strong>first syllable</strong>” (8.1, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nouns: PRE-sent, TI-ger, BREAK-fast (8.1); SAM-ples, CAR-ton, CON-tent (8.4, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Adjectives: HAND-some, EA-sy, HAP-py (8.1); RAI-ny (8.4).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two-syllable verbs and prepositions</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“With most disyllabic verbs, stress is on the <strong>last syllable</strong>” (8.1, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>). Lecture 8.4 adds prepositions (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Verbs: in-CREASE, be-GIN (8.1); re-LAX, re-CEIVE, di-RECT (8.4).</li>\n<li>Preposition: a-MONG (8.4).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The 8.4 slide sums up: “About 80% of two-syllable words get their stress on the first syllable. There are exceptions to this rule, but very few nouns and adjectives get stress on their second syllable. Verbs and prepositions usually get stress placed on the second syllable, but there are exceptions to this too.”</p>\n<h3 id=\"same-spelling-noun-or-verb\">Same spelling, noun or verb</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Stress tells a noun from a verb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“To differentiate between a <strong>noun and a verb</strong> with the same spelling, <strong>stress position changes</strong>” (8.1, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Noun: first syllable</th>\n<th>Verb: second syllable</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a DE-crease</td>\n<td>to de-CREASE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>an OB-ject</td>\n<td>to ob-JECT</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<p>Short sentences to hear the difference (the sentences are mine):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>There was a <strong>DE</strong>crease in sales. / Sales de<strong>CREASE</strong> in winter.</li>\n<li>What is that <strong>OB</strong>ject? / I ob<strong>JECT</strong> to this plan.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Noun or verb: object (Lecture 8.5’s practice at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdteKj-oE5I&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Split the word: ob-ject, two syllables.</li>\n<li>As a <strong>noun</strong> (a thing), the two-syllable noun pattern applies: stress on the first syllable, <strong>OB</strong>-ject. The 8.5 slide writes it 'Object, with the stress mark before the stressed syllable.</li>\n<li>As a <strong>verb</strong> (to protest), the verb pattern applies: stress on the second syllable, ob-<strong>JECT</strong>, written Ob’ject.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> an OB-ject (noun), to ob-JECT (verb). Same letters, different words; only the stress shows which.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The principle of economy</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Why can one word work as both a noun and a verb? Because the stress keeps the two apart, the language does not need two different words. The lecture calls this the <strong>principle of economy</strong> in the structure of language: getting more out of less. Applying it is “one of the specific features of human mind”, and such patterns develop in our subconscious as we learn a language (8.1, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"words-built-from-a-one-syllable-word\">Words built from a one-syllable word</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Stress stays on the original word</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“When a noun or adjective stems from a one-syllable word (for example <em>break</em> and <em>friend</em>), the stress usually stays on the syllable of the <strong>original word</strong>” (8.1, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>break → <strong>BREAK</strong>-able</li>\n<li>friend → <strong>FRIEND</strong>-ly</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Here <em>-able</em> and <em>-ly</em> are <strong>suffixes</strong>: parts added at the end of a word to make a new word. The new word is a <strong>derived</strong> word, and the word it comes from is its <strong>stem</strong>. Most words derived this way keep the stress on the stem.</p>\n<h3 id=\"compound-words-phrasal-verbs-and-reflexive-pronouns\">Compound words, phrasal verbs and reflexive pronouns</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Compound nouns: the first part</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>compound noun</strong> is “two words merged into one” (8.1), “a noun made out of two nouns in order to form one word” (8.4). “In a compound noun, the <strong>first word</strong> usually takes on the stress.”</p>\n<ul>\n<li>BOOK-shop, NOTE-book (8.1, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>)</li>\n<li>SEA-food, TOOTH-paste (8.4, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>)</li>\n<li>DIN-ing room (8.5: “most compound words have primary stress on the first element”)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Compound adjectives: the second word</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>compound adjective</strong> is “an adjective composed of at least two words. In compound adjectives, the stress is placed within the <strong>second word</strong>” (8.4, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>rock-SOLID, sparkling-RED</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Phrasal verbs: the second word</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Phrasal verbs are words made from a verb and preposition. In phrasal verbs, the <strong>second word</strong> gets the stress (the preposition)” (8.4, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>black OUT, break DOWN, look OUT</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Sentences (mine): <em>Look <strong>OUT</strong>! A car is coming.</em> <em>My old car may break <strong>DOWN</strong> on the way.</em></p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Reflexive pronouns: the second syllable</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Reflexive pronouns show that the action affects the person who performs the action. For example: I hit myself.” “The <strong>second syllable</strong> usually takes the stress” (8.4, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>my-SELF, them-SELVES, our-SELVES</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Noun and phrasal verb (my observation)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The same two words can make a compound noun or a phrasal verb, and the stress follows the pattern: a <strong>BREAK</strong>down (noun, first part) but to break <strong>DOWN</strong> (phrasal verb, second word). It is the OB-ject / ob-JECT idea again.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"endings-that-decide-the-stress\">Endings that decide the stress</h3>\n<p>A <strong>suffix</strong> can tell you where the stress goes. The lectures give four groups. The word <strong>penultimate</strong> means “second last”.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Second-last syllable: -tion, -sion, -cian and -ic</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 8.1 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“The words ending in -TION, -SION and -CIAN are usually stressed on the <strong>second last syllable</strong>”: e-du-CA-tion, per-MIS-sion, phy-SI-cian.</li>\n<li>“Words ending with -IC are usually stressed on the second last syllable”: rea-LIS-tic, stra-TE-gic.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are “quite productive patterns”: English makes many words with these endings.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Where is the stress in education? (the lecture’s word at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Split the word: e-du-ca-tion, four syllables.</li>\n<li>The ending is -tion, so the stress goes on the second last syllable.</li>\n<li>Count from the end: <em>tion</em> is the last, <em>ca</em> is the second last.</li>\n<li>Stress <em>ca</em>: e-du-<strong>CA</strong>-tion. “Not on the last one … but the syllable before that.”</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> e-du-CA-tion.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The syllable right before the suffix</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 8.4 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>): many suffixes “take stress on the <strong>syllable right before the suffix</strong>. This applies to words of all syllable lengths.” The suffixes on the slide are:</p>\n<p><em>-able, -ary, -cian, -cial, -ery, -graphy, -ial, -ian, -ible, -ic, -ical, -ics, -ion, -ity, -ium, -imum, -logy, -tal.</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide’s examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>), and two from Lecture 8.5:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Suffix</th>\n<th>Words, stressed syllable in capitals</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>-able</td>\n<td>un-der-STAND-a-ble, DU-ra-ble</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ary</td>\n<td>PRI-ma-ry, DI-a-ry</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ible</td>\n<td>VIS-i-ble, TER-ri-ble</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ical</td>\n<td>MAG-i-cal, CRIT-i-cal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ics</td>\n<td>ge-NET-ics, pae-di-AT-rics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ion (8.5)</td>\n<td>ex-am-i-NA-tion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ial (8.5)</td>\n<td>me-MO-ri-al</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Stress in understandable (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Split the word: un-der-stand-a-ble. “It’s a five-syllable word.”</li>\n<li>Find the suffix: <em>-able</em>.</li>\n<li>Find the syllable right before the suffix: <em>stand</em>.</li>\n<li>Stress it: un-der-<strong>STAND</strong>-a-ble.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> un-der-STAND-a-ble. The word is long, but the suffix points straight at the stress.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two rules, one answer</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>For <em>-tion</em> and <em>-ic</em>, Lecture 8.1’s “second last syllable” and Lecture 8.4’s “syllable right before the suffix” give the same syllable, because <em>-tion</em> and <em>-ic</em> are one syllable each: e-du-<strong>CA</strong>-tion, rea-<strong>LIS</strong>-tic.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">First syllable: -er, -or, -ly, and some other endings</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 8.4 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>): for words ending in <em>-er</em>, <em>-or</em> and <em>-ly</em>, “stress is placed on the <strong>first syllable</strong>”: DI-rec-tor, OR-der-ly, MA-nag-er.</p>\n<p>The next slide, “Words ending in consonants and y” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>), also stresses the first syllable: RA-ri-ty, OP-ti-mal, GRA-di-ent, CON-tain-er.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Exceptions inside the examples</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lectures warn that there are many exceptions, and two of these slide examples are exceptions themselves. Dictionaries stress <em>director</em> and <em>container</em> on the <strong>second</strong> syllable: di-REC-tor, con-TAIN-er. <em>Orderly</em>, <em>manager</em>, <em>rarity</em>, <em>optimal</em> and <em>gradient</em> do follow the first-syllable pattern. Use a pattern as a first guess, and check new words in a dictionary.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The ending itself: -ee, -oo, -ese, -ique, -ette</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>“Words ending with -ee or -oo are usually stressed on the <strong>last syllable</strong>”: guaran-TEE, sham-POO (8.1, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>). These endings are syllables by themselves and carry the stress.</li>\n<li>For words ending in <em>-ee, -ese, -ique</em> and <em>-ette</em>, “the primary stress is placed on <strong>the suffix</strong>” (8.4, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>): a-GREE, gaur-en-TEE (guarantee), u-NIQUE, phy-SIQUE.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slides give no examples for <em>-ese</em> and <em>-ette</em>; two of mine: Ja-pa-NESE, cas-SETTE.</p>\n<h3 id=\"prefixes\">Prefixes</h3>\n<p>A <strong>prefix</strong> is a part added at the <strong>start</strong> of a word: <em>un-, in-, pre-, ex-, mis-</em>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Prefixes and stress</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 8.4 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>): “Usually, prefixes do not take the stress of a word. There are a few exceptions to this rule, however, like: <em>un, in, pre, ex</em> and <em>mis</em>, which are all stressed in their prefix.”</p>\n<ul>\n<li>in: IN-efficient</li>\n<li>pre: PRE-ar-range</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Main stress and lighter stress</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Dictionaries show two kinds of stress mark: ˈ before the <strong>main</strong> (primary) stress, and a low mark ˌ before a <strong>lighter</strong> (secondary) stress. The 8.5 slides use both: <em>neurology</em> is /ˌnjʊə-ˈrɒ-lə-dʒi/, with the lighter stress on <em>neu</em> and the main stress on <em>ro</em>. For <em>inefficient</em> and <em>prearrange</em>, dictionaries put the main stress later (in-ef-FI-cient, pre-ar-RANGE) and the lighter stress on the prefix. That lighter stress is what you hear on these prefixes.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"when-you-are-not-sure\">When you are not sure</h3>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">When in doubt, do not stress</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The last 8.1 slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8gud9XJsL6k&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>): “When in doubt, do not stress. It is better to not stress than stress the wrong syllable.” When none of the patterns applies, Indian English speakers usually stress no syllable at all, and that is fine. A stress placed on purpose on the wrong syllable “doesn’t sound good”, and sometimes sounds awkward. If you are not sure, speak naturally, the way we speak English in India.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Use a dictionary</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 8.4 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SrSSSEHWxk&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>): “Dictionaries are great tools for learning word stress. For every word, the dictionary indicates where the stress goes. For example by placing an apostrophe before the stress.” The slide’s example: <em>animation</em>, /anɪˈmeɪʃ(ə)n/. The mark ˈ stands before <em>meɪ</em>, so the word is a-ni-<strong>MA</strong>-tion, just as the <em>-tion</em> pattern predicts. (See also <a href=\"#/english1/w03/dictionary-and-thesaurus\">dictionaries in Week 3</a>.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"plurals-s-z-and-iz\">Plurals: /s/, /z/ and /iz/</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 8.2. We use plurals all the time: two books, many friends. In writing, a plural adds <em>-s</em>, <em>-es</em> or <em>-ies</em>. The lecture asks us to stop looking at the spelling and <strong>listen</strong> instead. Then the whole system becomes one simple rule about the <strong>last sound</strong> of the word.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Noun, singular, plural</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>“Nouns are <strong>naming words</strong>. All words denoting/referring to names are nouns” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). This covers the usual “name of a person, place or thing”.</li>\n<li>A <strong>singular</strong> noun denotes <strong>one</strong> of something.</li>\n<li>A <strong>plural</strong> noun denotes <strong>more than one</strong>, in English and in many other languages (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Plurality</strong> is a feature of nouns. The <strong>plural marker</strong> is the part that shows the plural, such as the <em>-s</em> of <em>dogs</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"spelling-first-then-sound\">Spelling first, then sound</h3>\n<p>The slide <strong>Singular and Plural Nouns</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>) lists seven common nouns. In spelling, the plurals add <em>s</em> (dogs, friends, judges, books, caps), <em>es</em> (benches) or <em>ies</em> (babies) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). There are more spelling patterns too, as in <em>knife</em> (<em>knives</em>).</p>\n<p>Now say each word and listen to its <strong>last sound</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>), and to the sound of the plural marker (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>). The slide <strong>Plural Marking sounds</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Singular</th>\n<th>Its last sound</th>\n<th>Plural</th>\n<th>Plural sound</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>dog</td>\n<td>/g/</td>\n<td>dogs</td>\n<td>/z/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>friend</td>\n<td>/d/</td>\n<td>friends</td>\n<td>/z/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>judge</td>\n<td>/dʒ/</td>\n<td>judges</td>\n<td>/iz/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>bench</td>\n<td>/tʃ/</td>\n<td>benches</td>\n<td>/iz/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>baby</td>\n<td>a vowel (the ee sound)</td>\n<td>babies</td>\n<td>/z/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>book</td>\n<td>/k/</td>\n<td>books</td>\n<td>/s/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cap</td>\n<td>/p/</td>\n<td>caps</td>\n<td>/s/</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“Plural markers in English appear to be sounds like: /z/, /iz/, and /s/.” (Dictionaries write /iz/ as /ɪz/, with the short vowel of <em>sit</em>.)</p>\n<p>Two groups appear (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>): some words add <strong>/z/</strong>, some add <strong>/s/</strong>. The /iz/ of <em>judges</em> and <em>benches</em> is /z/ with a short vowel in front: after the last sound of <em>judge</em> (/dʒ/) or <em>bench</em> (/tʃ/), a /z/ straight after would make a string of consonants that is hard to say, so we put in a short vowel to break it up (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>). So “there are only two plural marking sounds in English”, /s/ and /z/, and the main job is knowing which one to add. “This is the most critical part of learning plurality in English.”</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-rule\">The rule</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The plural sound</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide <strong>Understanding Plurals in English</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>“In words ending in sounds /p/, /t/, and /k/, the plural markers sound as <strong>/s/</strong>.”</li>\n<li>“Every where else the plural marker in English is <strong>/z/</strong>.”</li>\n<li>“There does not seem to be much of a difference between /z/ and /iz/.” The vowel of /iz/ comes in “just to break the continuity of two consonant sounds”, as in <em>judges</em>, <em>benches</em> and <em>roses</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<p>“Look how simple it is” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>): if the singular does not end in /p/, /t/ or /k/, you can safely say /z/. This is about <strong>sound</strong>: in spelling we still write <em>s</em>.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Plural sounds of clocks, windows and roses (the lecture’s words at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>Clock</em>: the last sound is /k/, one of /p/, /t/, /k/. So the plural sounds <strong>/s/</strong>: clocks.</li>\n<li><em>Window</em>: the last sound is a vowel (the o sound), not /p/, /t/ or /k/. So the plural sounds <strong>/z/</strong>: windows. We write <em>s</em>, but we say /z/ (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Rose</em>: the last sound is already /z/. Adding /z/ straight after it is hard to say, so a short vowel comes in: roses, with <strong>/iz/</strong>, the same thing that happens in <em>judges</em> and <em>benches</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> clocks /s/, windows /z/, roses /iz/. The lecture’s other words follow the same rule: <em>cat</em> → cats /s/; <em>computer</em>, <em>bottle</em> → computers, bottles /z/.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">The letter s is not always the sound /s/</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Spelling adds <em>s</em> to <em>dogs</em> and <em>friends</em>, but the sound is /z/. Do not say <em>dogs</em> with a hissing /s/ at the end; say it as it is spoken, with /z/ (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"why-s-and-z-and-why-p-t-k\">Why /s/ and /z/, and why /p/, /t/, /k/</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">/s/ and /z/: one difference, voicing</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Both are <strong>fricatives</strong>: the air is pushed through a narrow gap and makes a hissing sound (<a href=\"#/english1/w01/the-english-consonant-chart\">Week 1</a>). The only difference is <strong>voicing</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>): /s/ is voiceless, /z/ is voiced. Put your fingers on the front of your throat. Say <em>sss</em>: no vibration. Say <em>zzz</em>: you feel the vocal cords vibrate. In one way they are the same sound with a difference of voicing; the lecture treats them as two sounds.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>What do /p/, /t/ and /k/ have in common (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>)? They come from three different places: /p/ is <strong>bilabial</strong> (both lips), /t/ is <strong>alveolar</strong> (the tongue tip on the ridge behind the upper teeth), and /k/ is <strong>velar</strong> (the back of the tongue on the soft palate). But all three are <strong>stops</strong>: the air is fully blocked and then let go. “We will see more importance of these sounds being stop sounds” later: that is aspiration, the next topic.</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Old plurals</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Teeth</em> (from <em>tooth</em>), <em>oxen</em> (from <em>ox</em>) and <em>children</em> (from <em>child</em>) do not follow the rule (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>). They are remnants of <strong>Old English</strong>. Languages change over time, but some old forms stay. Maybe one day people will say <em>childs</em>; for now, the plural of <em>child</em> is <em>children</em>. Set these aside and learn them as they are.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Beyond the lecture</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s rule covers the common cases. Dictionaries describe two more details: /s/ also comes after the other voiceless sounds /f/ and /θ/ (<em>cliffs</em>, <em>months</em>), and /iz/ comes after every hissing sound, /s, z, ʃ, ʒ, tʃ, dʒ/ (<em>buses</em>, <em>dishes</em>). The examples are mine.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s plural exercise</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Close your eyes and think of 15 to 20 nouns, or just look around: chairs, computers, TV, plates, clocks, door, window (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>). For each one, say the singular, find its last sound, and decide: /s/ or /z/? Then do the same with 20 nouns from something you read (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=42hufxqiOAk&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"aspiration\">Aspiration</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 8.3 puts three ideas together. Its slide <strong>Elements of words</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aspiration</strong> – a feature of sound;</li>\n<li><strong>Syllable</strong> – a unit of words;</li>\n<li><strong>Stress</strong> – a feature of vowels in a syllable.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When we combine syllables and stress, we notice that “certain sounds in certain syllables are also aspirated” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). To understand aspiration you need the two earlier topics.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Why care? To communicate effectively, our language must be flawless (grammatical) and must “sound proper”: fluency, appropriateness and correctness. The effort we put into improving adds to our <strong>confidence</strong>, and confidence makes us sound better (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Aspiration</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Aspiration</strong> is “that extra puff of air that you produce when you pronounce certain sounds” (8.5, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdteKj-oE5I&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>). It is written with a small raised h, which “indicates additional flow of air” (8.3, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>): [pʰ], [tʰ], [kʰ]. A sound with the puff is <strong>aspirated</strong>; without it, <strong>unaspirated</strong>. Test: hold your palm in front of your mouth (<a href=\"#/english1/w01/aspiration-and-voicing\">Week 1</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"take-a-piece-of-cake\">Take a piece of cake</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>): <em>Take a piece of cake.</em> Some speakers say it with a puff on the first sound of <em>take</em>, <em>piece</em> and <em>cake</em>. The slide writes: <em>Tʰake a pʰiece of cʰake</em> (the cʰ stands for the k sound with a puff).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>This is how many speakers of British, American, Australian and Canadian English speak (with variation inside each) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</li>\n<li>In <strong>Indian English</strong> we usually do <strong>not</strong> aspirate these sounds. “That’s perfectly fine; there is nothing problematic or bad about Indian variety of English” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Understanding the pattern first lets us see the difference between the varieties, and learn it if we want to. Copying without understanding “will make you sound ridiculous” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-voiceless-stops-p-t-k\">The voiceless stops /p/, /t/, /k/</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Voiceless stops</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide <strong>[p], [t], [k] … Voiceless stop sounds</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sound</th>\n<th>Place</th>\n<th>No puff (−asp, −voice)</th>\n<th>Puff (+asp, −voice)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>p</td>\n<td>bilabial: both lips</td>\n<td>[p]</td>\n<td>[pʰ]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>t</td>\n<td>alveolar: tongue tip on the ridge behind the upper teeth</td>\n<td>[t]</td>\n<td>[tʰ]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>k</td>\n<td>velar: back of the tongue on the soft palate</td>\n<td>[k]</td>\n<td>[kʰ]</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stop</strong>: in making these sounds there is “a total blockage at their respective places of articulation and sudden release of the flow of air” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Voiceless</strong> (−voice): the vocal cords do not vibrate.</li>\n<li><strong>−asp</strong> means unaspirated, <strong>+asp</strong> aspirated. Aspiration changes only −asp to +asp: the sound stays voiceless.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"one-syllable-words\">One-syllable words</h3>\n<p>The slide <strong>Some English Words</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>First sound</th>\n<th>Words</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>p</td>\n<td>pen, pin, pan, park</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>t</td>\n<td>tan, truck, trick, team</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>k</td>\n<td>car, can, king, crush</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>All these words have one syllable and begin with /p/, /t/ or /k/. When we say them, the first sound gets aspirated: [pʰ]en, [tʰ]an, [kʰ]ar. <em>Truck</em> and <em>trick</em> have the pattern <strong>CCVC</strong> (C = consonant, V = vowel): t and r form a cluster at the start, and the t is still aspirated.</p>\n<p>Look at the k at the <strong>end</strong> of <em>park</em>, <em>truck</em> and <em>trick</em>: it is <strong>not</strong> aspirated, because it is not at the start (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>). So “aspiration of k is not uniform”: the same sound gets a puff in one position and not in another.</p>\n<p>From one-syllable words we are tempted to say: stops get aspirated at the start of a word. “Keep in mind this is half of the story” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"longer-words-the-stressed-syllable\">Longer words: the stressed syllable</h3>\n<p>The slide <strong>Aspiration in stressed syllable</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>). The slides respell each word with the stressed syllable in capitals and the puff marked, like /pa TʰAE to/ for <em>potato</em>; below, the words are split in normal spelling.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Syllables, stressed in capitals</th>\n<th>Puff on</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>potato</td>\n<td>po-TA-to (3)</td>\n<td>the t of TA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>hotel</td>\n<td>ho-TEL (2)</td>\n<td>the t of TEL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nepal</td>\n<td>ne-PAL (2)</td>\n<td>the p of PAL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>intense</td>\n<td>in-TENSE (2)</td>\n<td>the t of TENSE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>intention</td>\n<td>in-TEN-tion (3)</td>\n<td>the t of TEN</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Aspiration in the stressed syllable</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In words of two or more syllables, /p/, /t/ and /k/ get aspirated “not at the initial position of the word”, but “at the initial position of the <strong>stressed syllable</strong>” (8.3, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>). A stop at the start of an unstressed syllable stays unaspirated.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><em>Potato</em> shows it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>). Its p is at the start of a syllable too, but <em>po</em> is not stressed, so Lecture 8.3 gives the puff only to the t of the stressed <em>TA</em>. Why not to both? Aspirating two sounds in syllables “one after the other will not be possible, will not be easy in speaking” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</p>\n<p>The puff needs the <strong>start</strong> of the stressed syllable. In <em>deeper</em>, DEEP-er, the p is inside the stressed syllable but at its end, so it is not aspirated (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>).</p>\n<p>More of the lecture’s words (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Syllables, stressed in capitals</th>\n<th>Puff on</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>attention</td>\n<td>at-TEN-tion (3)</td>\n<td>t</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>retain</td>\n<td>re-TAIN (2)</td>\n<td>t</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>retention</td>\n<td>re-TEN-tion (3)</td>\n<td>t</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>impossible</td>\n<td>im-POS-si-ble (4)</td>\n<td>p</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>impatient</td>\n<td>im-PA-tient (3)</td>\n<td>p</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>department</td>\n<td>de-PART-ment (3)</td>\n<td>p</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>competition</td>\n<td>com-pe-TI-tion (4)</td>\n<td>the t of TI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>decrease (verb)</td>\n<td>de-CREASE (2)</td>\n<td>k</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>decrease (noun)</td>\n<td>DE-crease (2)</td>\n<td>none</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>institution</td>\n<td>in-sti-TU-tion (4)</td>\n<td>the t of TU</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Decrease as a verb and as a noun (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Both words are de-crease, two syllables, and the second syllable starts with the k sound (spelled c).</li>\n<li><strong>Verb</strong>: two-syllable verbs stress the second syllable, de-<strong>CREASE</strong>. The k sound is at the start of the stressed syllable, so it is aspirated: de-[kʰ]rease.</li>\n<li><strong>Noun</strong>: two-syllable nouns stress the first syllable, <strong>DE</strong>-crease. Now the k sound starts an <strong>unstressed</strong> syllable, so “even though we have a k sound at the initial position of this syllable, it is not aspirated” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> only the verb <em>to decrease</em> has an aspirated k. Stress decides where the puff goes.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The next slide shows the same thing from the other side: <strong>“Stress on 1st syllable – 2nd syllable unstressed and unaspirated”</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Syllables</th>\n<th>What happens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>purple</td>\n<td>PUR-ple</td>\n<td>first p aspirated; the p of <em>ple</em> not</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>practice</td>\n<td>PRAC-tice</td>\n<td>first p aspirated; the t of <em>tice</em> not</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>circle</td>\n<td>CIR-cle</td>\n<td>the k sound of <em>cle</em> not</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>turtle</td>\n<td>TUR-tle</td>\n<td>first t aspirated; the second t not</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sample</td>\n<td>SAM-ple</td>\n<td>the p of <em>ple</em> not</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>temple</td>\n<td>TEM-ple</td>\n<td>the t aspirated; the p of <em>ple</em> not</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>happy</td>\n<td>HAP-py</td>\n<td>the p not: it is not at the start of a stressed syllable</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>And <strong>“2nd syllable stressed – first sound aspirated”</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>): pla-TON-ic, i-CON-ic, a-TOM-ic (the t or k of the stressed syllable is aspirated), com-PAS-sion and com-PLETE (the p is aspirated). In <em>compassion</em> and <em>complete</em> the k is at the start, “but not aspirated, because these are not stressed syllables” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The rule, said backwards</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“/p/, /t/, /k/ get aspirated at the initial position of a stressed syllable” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>). In a one-syllable word there is no question of stress: its only syllable starts where the word starts. That is why, in <em>take</em>, <em>piece</em> and <em>cake</em>, the puff seemed to belong to the start of the word.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">After s: no puff</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The note on the slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IoprN0REcgs&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>): <em>school</em> – skuul, <em>cool</em> – kuul. In <em>school</em> the k comes after s, so it is not at the start and it is not aspirated. In <em>cool</em> the k starts the word, so for some speakers it is aspirated: [kʰ]ool.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-rules-to-remember\">The rules to remember</h3>\n<p>Lecture 8.5 collects the rules on one slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdteKj-oE5I&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>):</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Rules to remember</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>“Voiceless stops are aspirated at the beginning of a word, and at the beginning of a stressed syllable.”</li>\n<li>“Voiceless stops are unaspirated at the beginning of an unstressed syllable. They’re also unaspirated in any other position, like at the end of a syllable or the end of a word.”</li>\n<li>“Even if a syllable is stressed, a voiceless stop is unaspirated if it follows [s].”</li>\n<li>“Voiced stops are never aspirated. They’re always unaspirated.” (The <strong>voiced stops</strong> are b, d and g.)</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Which words have aspirated sounds: stop, pot, cot, oppose, tame? (Lecture 8.5’s practice at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdteKj-oE5I&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>Pot</em>: p is a voiceless stop at the beginning of the word → aspirated, [pʰ]ot.</li>\n<li><em>Cot</em>: k (spelled c) at the beginning of the word → aspirated, [kʰ]ot.</li>\n<li><em>Oppose</em>: split op-pose; the stress is on the second syllable, op-<strong>POSE</strong>. The p starts the stressed syllable → aspirated (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdteKj-oE5I&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Tame</em>: t at the beginning of the word → aspirated, [tʰ]ame.</li>\n<li><em>Stop</em>: the t is in the stressed (only) syllable, but it follows s → <strong>not</strong> aspirated (rule 3). Compare <em>top</em>, where the t starts the word and is aspirated (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdteKj-oE5I&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> pot, cot, oppose and tame have aspirated sounds; stop does not.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two lectures, one difference</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The two lectures agree on one-syllable words, on stressed syllables, on <em>s</em> + stop, and on final stops. They differ only on a /p/, /t/ or /k/ that <strong>starts a word but starts an unstressed syllable</strong>, like the p of <em>potato</em> or the k of <em>compassion</em>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lecture 8.3 says it is <strong>not</strong> aspirated: only the stressed syllable gets the puff (po-[tʰ]a-to).</li>\n<li>Lecture 8.5’s rule 1 says stops are aspirated “at the beginning of a word, <strong>and</strong> at the beginning of a stressed syllable”, which gives that p a puff too.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Phonetics books describe such a sound as aspirated, but more weakly than at the start of a stressed syllable. So the strong, clear puff is at the start of a stressed syllable; a stop that starts a word before an unstressed vowel gets less.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"practice-from-lecture-8-5\">Practice from Lecture 8.5</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 8.5 is a practice session: four sets of questions, each paused for you to try and then explained. They use everything above. Try each one before you read the answer.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"how-many-syllables\">How many syllables?</h3>\n<p>The words (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdteKj-oE5I&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>) and the slide’s answers (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdteKj-oE5I&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Slide’s split</th>\n<th>Syllables</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>lexicographer</td>\n<td>Lek-si-ko-gra-fe</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>magistrate</td>\n<td>Ma-gis-treit</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>civilize</td>\n<td>Ci-vi-laiz</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>is</td>\n<td>Is</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>boys</td>\n<td>Boiz</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>know</td>\n<td>Know</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>linguistics</td>\n<td>Lin-gwis-tics</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sorry</td>\n<td>So-ri</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>episode</td>\n<td>e-pi-sode</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>neurology</td>\n<td>Nu-ro-lo-gi</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">How many syllables in lexicographer? (the lecture’s at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdteKj-oE5I&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Split by vowel <strong>sounds</strong>: one vowel sound per syllable, and only one.</li>\n<li>lek – si – ko – gra – fe: five vowel sounds.</li>\n<li>So five syllables. The slide’s IPA also marks a lighter stress on <em>lek</em> and the main stress on the third syllable, <em>ko</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 5 syllables.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The tricky one is <em>boys</em>: two vowel letters, one vowel sound, so one syllable (see the warning in <a href=\"#counting-syllables\">Counting syllables</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"consonant-clusters\">Consonant clusters</h3>\n<p>The question (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdteKj-oE5I&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>): give three words for each cluster pattern. <strong>Word initial</strong> means at the start of a word, <strong>word final</strong> at the end. C stands for a consonant <strong>sound</strong>, so CC is two consonant sounds together, and CCC three. The lecturer’s answers (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdteKj-oE5I&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Lecture’s words</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>initial CC</td>\n<td>place (pl), tray (tr), dwell (dw)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>final CC</td>\n<td>pump (mp), help (lp), adopt (pt)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>initial CCC</td>\n<td>spring (spr), straight (str), scream (scr)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>final CCC</td>\n<td>hands (nds), solves (lvz), adopts (pts)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">The silent e of solves</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Solves</em> has the letter e before the final s, so it seems to have a vowel there. It does not: the e is silent, and the word ends in the sounds l, v and z. “Do not be misguided by vowel letters, but instead focus on the consonant sounds” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdteKj-oE5I&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Plurals in the clusters (my observation)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Two of the final clusters are plurals, and the plural rule tells you their last sound: <em>hand</em> ends in /d/, so <em>hands</em> ends in /z/ (n, d, z); <em>adopt</em> ends in /t/, so <em>adopts</em> ends in /s/ (p, t, s). The three initial clusters all fit the <a href=\"#/english1/w01/clusters-of-three-consonants\">three-consonant template</a> from Week 1: s, then p, t or k, then r or l.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"mark-the-stress\">Mark the stress</h3>\n<p>The words (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdteKj-oE5I&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>) and the answers (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdteKj-oE5I&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdteKj-oE5I&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>), with the stress mark ’ before the stressed syllable as on the slide:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>object (noun)</td>\n<td>'Object: OB-ject</td>\n<td>two-syllable noun: first syllable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>object (verb)</td>\n<td>Ob’ject: ob-JECT</td>\n<td>two-syllable verb: second syllable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>examination</td>\n<td>Exami’nation: ex-am-i-NA-tion</td>\n<td>-ion: second last syllable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>memorial</td>\n<td>Me’morial: me-MO-ri-al</td>\n<td>-ial: the syllable before the suffix</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>dining room</td>\n<td>'Dining room: DIN-ing room</td>\n<td>compound: first element</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Stress in memorial (the lecture’s at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdteKj-oE5I&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Split the word: me-mo-ri-al.</li>\n<li>Find the suffix: <em>-ial</em> (it is on the 8.4 list).</li>\n<li>The syllable before the suffix is <em>mo</em> (the lecturer says <em>mor</em>).</li>\n<li>Stress it: me-<strong>MO</strong>-ri-al.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> me-MO-ri-al.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"which-words-are-aspirated\">Which words are aspirated?</h3>\n<p>This is the example worked under <a href=\"#the-rules-to-remember\">The rules to remember</a>: <em>pot</em>, <em>cot</em>, <em>oppose</em> and <em>tame</em> are aspirated; <em>stop</em> is not.</p>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Syllables</strong> (8.1, 8.4, 8.5): a syllable is a unit of a word: a nucleus (almost always a vowel sound) with optional consonant margins. No syllable, and no word, without a vowel. Count vowel <strong>sounds</strong>, one per syllable (<em>boys</em> = 1). Jaw-drop test. Words of five or more syllables are rare.</p>\n<p><strong>Stress</strong> (8.1, 8.4): prominence: one syllable said with more force, louder and longer. Golden rules: one stress per word; only vowels are stressed; many exceptions. Written WA-ter, a-ni-MA-tion; dictionaries mark ˈ (main) and ˌ (lighter).</p>\n<p><strong>Patterns</strong> (about 80% of words): two-syllable nouns and adjectives → 1st (TI-ger, HAP-py); two-syllable verbs and prepositions → 2nd (be-GIN, a-MONG); noun/verb pairs (an OB-ject, to ob-JECT). Derived from a one-syllable word → the stem (BREAK-able). Compound nouns → 1st part (BOOK-shop); compound adjectives → 2nd word (rock-SOLID); phrasal verbs → 2nd word (break DOWN); reflexives → 2nd syllable (my-SELF). -tion, -sion, -cian, -ic → second last (e-du-CA-tion). -able, -ary, -cian, -cial, -ery, -graphy, -ial, -ian, -ible, -ic, -ical, -ics, -ion, -ity, -ium, -imum, -logy, -tal → the syllable before (un-der-STAND-a-ble). -er, -or, -ly → 1st (MA-nag-er; but di-REC-tor). -ee, -oo, -ese, -ique, -ette → the ending (guaran-TEE). Prefixes: usually unstressed. When in doubt, do not stress.</p>\n<p><strong>Plurals</strong> (8.2): listen to the last sound. After /p/, /t/, /k/ → /s/; everywhere else → /z/; after the last sound of judge, bench, rose, a short vowel comes in → /iz/. /s/ and /z/ differ only in voicing. Old plurals: teeth, oxen, children.</p>\n<p><strong>Aspiration</strong> (8.3, 8.5): a puff of air, [pʰ] [tʰ] [kʰ], on the voiceless stops /p/, /t/, /k/ at the start of a stressed syllable (in a one-syllable word, at its start). No puff at the start of an unstressed syllable (DE-crease), at the end (park), or after /s/ (stop, school). Voiced stops b, d, g: never. Indian English usually does not aspirate: a variety, not an error.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Lecture 8.1 asks which syllable is prominent in <em>tiger</em>, <em>between</em>, <em>police</em> and <em>marriage</em>. Use the patterns, then compare with a dictionary.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>Tiger</em> is a two-syllable <strong>noun</strong> → first syllable: <strong>TI</strong>-ger. The 8.1 slide confirms it.</li>\n<li><em>Between</em> is a two-syllable <strong>preposition</strong> → second syllable: be-<strong>TWEEN</strong>. A dictionary agrees.</li>\n<li><em>Police</em> is a two-syllable <strong>noun</strong>, so the pattern predicts PO-lice. But a dictionary gives po-<strong>LICE</strong>: it is one of the “very few nouns” that take the stress on the second syllable (8.4).</li>\n<li><em>Marriage</em> is a two-syllable <strong>noun</strong> → <strong>MAR</strong>-riage. A dictionary agrees.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> TI-ger, be-TWEEN, po-LICE (an exception), MAR-riage. The patterns are a strong first guess, not a law.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Lecture 8.1 lists <em>education</em>, <em>invisible</em>, <em>infinity</em> and <em>aquarium</em> and leaves the stress to you. Use the suffix patterns of Lecture 8.4 to place it.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>education</em> ends in <strong>-tion</strong> → second last syllable: e-du-<strong>CA</strong>-tion.</li>\n<li><em>invisible</em> ends in <strong>-ible</strong> → the syllable right before it: in-<strong>VIS</strong>-i-ble.</li>\n<li><em>infinity</em> ends in <strong>-ity</strong> → the syllable right before it: in-<strong>FIN</strong>-i-ty.</li>\n<li><em>aquarium</em> ends in <strong>-ium</strong> → the syllable right before it: a-<strong>QUAR</strong>-i-um.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> e-du-CA-tion, in-VIS-i-ble, in-FIN-i-ty, a-QUAR-i-um. Each one agrees with a dictionary.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which pattern places the stress, and where? <em>toothbrush, yourself, give up, musician, referee</em> (the words are mine)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>toothbrush</em>: a <strong>compound noun</strong> → the first part: <strong>TOOTH</strong>-brush.</li>\n<li><em>yourself</em>: a <strong>reflexive pronoun</strong> → the second syllable: your-<strong>SELF</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>give up</em>: a <strong>phrasal verb</strong> (verb + preposition) → the second word: give <strong>UP</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>musician</em>: ends in <strong>-cian</strong> → the second last syllable: mu-<strong>SI</strong>-cian.</li>\n<li><em>referee</em>: ends in <strong>-ee</strong> → the ending itself: ref-e-<strong>REE</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Does the plural end in /s/, /z/ or /iz/? <em>cups, beds, trees, desks, bridges</em> (the words are mine)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Say the singular and find its last sound.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>cup</em>: last sound /p/ → <strong>/s/</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>bed</em>: last sound /d/, not /p/, /t/ or /k/ → <strong>/z/</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>tree</em>: last sound a vowel → <strong>/z/</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>desk</em>: last sound /k/ → <strong>/s/</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>bridge</em>: last sound /dʒ/, as in <em>judge</em> → a short vowel comes in → <strong>/iz/</strong>, like <em>judges</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> cups /s/, beds /z/, trees /z/, desks /s/, bridges /iz/. The spelling adds <em>s</em> or <em>es</em> every time; only the sound tells the three apart.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which /p/, /t/ and /k/ sounds get a puff of air? <em>pay, spy, upper, attack, good</em> (the words are mine)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>pay</em>: p starts the word and its only syllable → <strong>aspirated</strong>, [pʰ]ay.</li>\n<li><em>spy</em>: the p follows s → <strong>not</strong> aspirated (rule 3 of Lecture 8.5).</li>\n<li><em>upper</em>: UP-per; the p starts the <strong>unstressed</strong> second syllable → <strong>not</strong> aspirated, like the p of <em>happy</em>.</li>\n<li><em>attack</em>: at-TACK; the t starts the <strong>stressed</strong> syllable → <strong>aspirated</strong>, at-[tʰ]ack. The final k is at the end of the word → <strong>not</strong> aspirated.</li>\n<li><em>good</em>: g and d are <strong>voiced</strong> stops → never aspirated.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> only the p of <em>pay</em> and the t of <em>attack</em>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Noun or verb? Mark the stress of the words in brackets. (a) She gave me a (present). (b) I will (present) my project tomorrow. (c) Prices (increase) every year. (The sentences are mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>(a) <em>present</em> is a <strong>noun</strong> here (a gift) → first syllable: <strong>PRE</strong>-sent, as on the 8.1 slide.</li>\n<li>(b) <em>present</em> is a <strong>verb</strong> here (to show) → second syllable: pre-<strong>SENT</strong>.</li>\n<li>(c) <em>increase</em> is a <strong>verb</strong> here → second syllable: in-<strong>CREASE</strong>, as on the 8.1 slide.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> PRE-sent, pre-SENT, in-CREASE. The position of the stress tells the listener which class the word belongs to: the principle of economy at work.</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
